{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke, etc., When you take the count of the children of Israel.</b> In every place he mentioned the lifting of the head in connection with the counting, for through the counting Israel is borne aloft with surpassing elevation above all the nations, for every count points to the particular distinction that each and every man possesses — for anything that is of no account, like straw and chaff, has no number, but Israel are likened to wheat, as it says (Song of Songs 7:3) ״בִּטְנֵךְ עֲרֵמַת חִטִּים״, for each heap in itself is a precious thing, for the owner of the field counts it, saying, 'I have so-and-so many heaps'; so too the belly of each and every one is a precious thing. And he used the word belly, lest you say that only from the side of the head is there a difference between Israel and the nations — for the name of HaShem is called specifically upon the head of Israel — but from the side of the belly they are all equal, for all alike use the needs of the body equally. Rather, even in their belly there is an aspect of holiness, for they do not eat except according to the Torah, as it is written (Proverbs 22:18) ״כִּי נָעִים כִּי תִשְׁמְרֵם בְּבִטְנֶךָ״, and it is written (Psalms 40:9) ״וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״. And this was so that one should not say that Israel have superiority only from the side of the head, in which dwells wisdom, for ״רַק עַם חָכָם וְנָבוֹן הַגּוֹי הַזֶּה״, but from the side of the use of matters of the body that go by way of the belly, they are like all the other nations. Rather, even their belly is like a heap of wheat, for it is ״סוּגָה בַּשּׁוֹשַׁנִּים״, that is to say: he eats according to a fence and a partition which the early ones established, who were likened to roses — שׁוֹנִים הֲלָכוֹת, laws that are the ways of the world. Therefore the mention of the belly adds to the head that he mentioned here." "And in this manner Israel are likened to the stars, as it is said of them (Isaiah 40:26) ״הַמּוֹצִיא בְמִסְפָּר צְבָאָם״, for that number is surely on account of their excellence, for without doubt the Holy One, blessed be He, knows their number. So the counting of the children of Israel after the episode of the calf resembles what is said after the sin of Reuben (Genesis 35:22) — ״וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁנֵים עָשָׂר״ (Genesis 35:23). Thus He informed us here that they were not rejected on account of the episode of the calf, but all of them retain the distinctions that the count indicates — that every man of Israel is attended by individual providence. Indeed, the count mentioned in the portion of בְּמִדְבַּר was because He came to cause His Shekhinah to dwell within each and every tribe, for this matter requires a count, for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not cause His Shekhinah to rest on fewer than twenty-two thousand (Yevamot 64a), as will be explained in its place with the help of HaShem; and the count of the plains of Moab was for the sake of the division of the land, of which it says ״לָרַב תַּרְבּוּ״, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:29.963159924Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}